INT. MOSSY TAVERN INSIDE A HOLLOW OAK - EVENING

Roots for rafters. Candles in acorn caps. A fire made of glowworms. The room smells of honey and rain.

LORD PERIWINKLE "PERRY" THISTLEDOWN (60s, dignified, a miniature crown of dew) sits at the table, pouring tea. His housekeeper, MARGERY NETTLEBY (50s, apron, spectacles), carries a tray of seed-cakes. The apprentice witch BRYONY OAKENSHAW (16, scorched sleeves) hunches over a book. And the innkeeper, GRIMBOLD "GRIM" HOLLOWSTONE (70s, enormous beard), polishes a mug that has been polished since spring.

PERRY
Bryony, my dear, put down the book.

BRYONY
I'm almost at the part, Lord Thistledown.

PERRY
Call me Perry. Everyone does.

MARGERY
Not everyone, my lord.

PERRY
Margery.

MARGERY
Perry.

GRIM
Tea's gone cold again, I'd say.

PERRY
It is always cold. You use your own tea.

GRIM
It's the principle.

BRYONY
(not looking up)
Lord Perry, what did the wizard say when he turned the king into a frog?

PERRY
I'm sure I don't know.

BRYONY
"Nothing. He'd already left."

MARGERY
That's a terrible joke, dear.

BRYONY
It's a terrible book.

GRIM
Would you like to hear a better one?

PERRY
Please, Grim, not the one about the dragon.

GRIM
It's a good one about the dragon.

MARGERY
It has no ending.

GRIM
That's the point of it.

She sets the tray down. He leans back. Bryony closes her book at last. The glowworm fire pulses once.

PERRY
It is a good evening.

MARGERY
It is.

GRIM
It's always a good evening, if nobody notices.

BRYONY
I notice.

PERRY
Then it is a good evening anyway.
